  "answer_text": "Opioids are derived from three main sources: **natural plants**, **laboratory chemical modifications of plant matter**, or **fully man-made chemical synthesis**. Additionally, the human body naturally produces its own internal opioids. \n\nThe four categories of opioids and where they come from are detailed below:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Natural Opioids (Opiates)\nThese are derived directly from the **opium poppy** plant (*Papaver somniferum*). \n* **The Source:** When the unripe seed pods of the opium poppy are lightly scratched, they secrete a milky, sticky latex sap. This raw opium sap contains natural chemical compounds called alkaloids.\n* **Examples:** \n  * **Morphine** (the primary active pain-relieving compound in opium)\n  * **Codeine**\n  * **Thebaine** (a minor poppy alkaloid used primarily as a starting material to make other drugs)\n\n### 2. Semi-Synthetic Opioids\nThese are created in a laboratory by chemically altering the natural alkaloids extracted from the opium poppy. Scientists modify these natural compounds to make them stronger, change how fast they absorb, or reduce certain side effects.\n* **The Source:** Formulated in a lab using natural poppy extracts (like morphine or thebaine) as the base ingredient.\n* **Examples:**\n  * **Heroin** (made by chemically processing morphine)\n  * **Oxycodone** (OxyContin, Percocet; derived from thebaine)\n  * **Hydrocodone** (Vicodin; derived from codeine or thebaine)\n  * **Hydromorphone** (Dilaudid)\n  * **Buprenorphine** (Suboxone)\n\n### 3. Fully Synthetic Opioids\nThese are entirely man-made in a laboratory using chemical synthesis. They do not contain any actual plant matter from the opium poppy. Instead, scientists design their molecular structures to mimic the shape of natural opiates so that they can bind to the exact same opioid receptors in the human brain and nervous system.\n* **The Source:** Fully artificial chemical creation.\n* **Examples:**\n  * **Fentanyl** (an extremely potent painkiller)\n  * **Methadone** (often used to treat opioid addiction)\n  * **Tramadol**\n\n### 4. Endogenous Opioids (Produced by the Body)\nYour own body has a built-in system that produces natural opioids to help you cope with pain, physical stress, and emotional trauma. \n* **The Source:** Specialized cells and glands in your brain and nervous system.\n* **Examples:**\n  * **Endorphins** (responsible for the \"runner's high\" or the rush of relief after an injury)\n  * **Enkephalins**\n  * **Dynorphins**",
